1️⃣ Understand the Core Idea

Your project is a Hospital Management System (HMS) designed to streamline hospital operations by connecting admins, doctors, and patients in one platform.

You should be able to explain the purpose clearly in one line:

“My Hospital Management System is a role-based web application that allows admins to manage doctors and patients, doctors to manage their appointments, and patients to easily book and track their appointments.”

⚙️ 2️⃣ Explain the Technology Stack

When asked “What technologies did you use?”, say:

“I used PHP for backend logic, MySQL as the database, and HTML, CSS, and Font Awesome for the front-end design. The system runs locally using XAMPP, which provides the Apache server and MySQL database.”

You can add:
Sessions are used for authentication and role-based access control.

SQL joins are used to connect multiple tables (users, doctors, patients, appointments).

The interface is responsive and user-friendly.

3️⃣ Explain the Database Structure

Show or describe your ER diagram (you can even sketch it if asked):

Tables:
users – stores common info (name, email, password, role)

doctors – linked to users via user_id

patients – linked to users via user_id

appointments – links patients and doctors with status tracking

Relationships:

One user can be a doctor or a patient.

Each appointment connects a doctor and a patient (many-to-one relationships).

Say something like:

“I separated doctors and patients into their own tables for scalability — this allows storing role-specific details like specialization or address without cluttering the users table.”

4️⃣ Describe the Login System & Roles

Explain how access control works:

“When a user logs in, the system checks their email and password, verifies it with password_verify(), and redirects them based on their role:

Admin → can view and approve all appointments.

Doctor → sees only their own appointments.

Patient → can book and track their appointments.”

Mention security briefly:

“Passwords are hashed for security, and sessions are used to maintain login states.

